# scenarios/3_stock.books: loaded by ledger_server into 3_stock.db the first time it is picked,
# and again by Start over. Amounts are in cents.
title	3. Stock: purchases (604) and the stock variation (609)
about	The Belgian way (PCMN/MAR): material bought for resale goes straight to 604 Purchases (achats, aankopen), a cost. At the year end the stock is counted, and what is still there goes back to 340 Stock, an asset, through 609 Stock variation (variation des stocks, voorraadwijziging), which lowers the cost of the year.
about	Here 5,000 of material is bought and 3,000 of it sold for 5,000; 2,000 is still in stock on 31 December. The costs are 5,000 of purchases less a 2,000 increase in stock: 3,000, so the profit is 2,000.
about	A stock increase leaves 609 with a credit balance, so the graph and the profit and loss show it like a revenue; the Belgian annual accounts keep it among the costs, as a negative amount next to 604.
about	Try it: if the stock went down instead, the variation would be a cost: an arrow from Stock to Stock variation.
currency	USD
account	100000	equity	Capital
account	550000	asset	Bank account
account	440000	liability	Suppliers
account	340000	asset	Stock
account	400001	asset	Customer 1
account	700000	revenue	Sales
account	604000	expense	Purchases
account	609000	expense	Stock variation
entry	2011-01-02	The founder puts in savings
posting	Capital	-500000
posting	Bank account	500000
entry	2011-01-15	Material bought on credit for 3,000
posting	Suppliers	-300000
posting	Purchases	300000
